# yes-toto — Project Overview

## What This Project Is

`yes-toto` is a faithful CLI replica of the GNU Core Utilities `yes` command.
It outputs a string (default: `y`) followed by a newline, in an infinite loop, to stdout — until killed by a signal or a broken pipe closes stdout.

## Binary Name
The compiled binary must always be named **`yes-toto`**. Never rename it.

## Scope — Keep It Minimal

This is intentionally a single-purpose tool. Do add:

- Flags or options (`--help`, `--version`, etc.)
- Message when the tool is called with unknown flags

**Do not add**:

- Interactive or TUI modes
- stdin reading
- Logging or debug output in normal operation
- Any feature that would break GNU `yes` behavioral compatibility

## Behavioral Contract

| Invocation | Output |
|---|---|
| `yes-toto` | prints `y\n` forever |
| `yes-toto hello` | prints `hello\n` forever |
| `yes-toto a b c` | prints `a b c\n` forever (args joined with space) |
| `yes-toto \| head -5` | prints 5 lines, exits cleanly on broken pipe |
| `yes-toto --version` or `yes-toto --v` shows version and information about the software (author, licence)
| `yes-toto --help` shows how to use the tool

